An enterprise integration may use one suitable driver for Oracle and another for SQL Server beneath middleware. The middleware can coordinate routing or expose a common application-facing contract. The drivers provide their supported backend access. Their responsibilities can overlap, but naming a layer middleware does not eliminate the need to inspect its driver and session behavior.

Prefer the repository''s established data-access boundary before introducing a second driver path. Trace direct callers, types, cleanup, and error handling. A new library is justified by a demonstrated unsupported need, not merely by the availability of another package. Drivers are most useful when their contracts are understood and reused consistently, preserving meaning and trusted behavior across the application-to-database boundary.
' or heading is distinct from 'Database Drivers');
update public.learning_topics set short_description='Understand database drivers through protocol access, API contracts, parameters, type conversion, transactions, diagnostics, and version compatibility rather than assuming automatic portability.',updated_at=now() where id=topic_uuid and short_description is distinct from 'Understand database drivers through protocol access, API contracts, parameters, type conversion, transactions, diagnostics, and version compatibility rather than assuming automatic portability.';
update public.practice_tests set title='MCQ Test 1',question_count=10,total_marks=10,pass_marks=4,duration_minutes=10,description='Concepts and fundamentals for Database Drivers.',instructions='Answer all ten questions. One mark each; no negative marking.',updated_at=now() where id='pt-dbms-sql-2-database-drivers' and (title<>'MCQ Test 1' or question_count<>10 or total_marks<>10);
